            <ArticleTitle>Cervical Spine Status of Pilots and Air-Controllers of Airborne Early Warning and Control Aircraft.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>476-480</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3357/AMHP.4734.2017</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Many countries have developed their own airborne early warning and control (AEW&amp;C) systems for use in surveying their territorial sky in real time. However, a review of the literature suggests that no studies have been conducted to analyze the cervical spine of pilots and air-controllers of AEW&amp;C aircraft.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The study subjects were 80 pilots and air-controllers of AEW&amp;C aircraft with a period of service of &gt; 1 yr and had data on physical examinations, simple radiographs and functional scores of the axial skeleton, and questionnaires about lifestyle and working conditions. Information about physical characteristics and experience of neck pain were collected. Functional scores including the neck disability index and short-form 36-item health survey were obtained. Radiological measurements were performed for the C2-7 Cobb angle and degree of forward head posture.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Of the 80 subjects, 33 (41.3%) had experienced neck pain and 63 (78.8%) had impaired cervical lordosis. The results of functional and radiological evaluations were not significantly different between pilots and air-controllers. In multivariate analysis, only the age was significantly related to the occurrence of impaired cervical lordosis. However, there were no significant factors related to the occurrence of neck pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The results of this study suggest that the working environment of pilots and air-controllers of AEW&amp;C aircraft has a negative effect on their cervical spine. Age seemed to be the most significant factor affecting the occurrence of impaired cervical lordosis in these subjects.Shin YH, Yun C, Han AH. Cervical spine status of pilots and air-controllers of airborne early warning and control aircraft. Aerosp Med Hum Perform. 2017; 88(5):476-480.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
